WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.701
-Our next guest is an
Oscar-nominated

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.467
writer and director
behind movies like "Looper,"

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:06.133
"Star Wars: The Last Jedi,"
and "Knives Out."

00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:09.067
His latest film, "Glass Onion,"
is streaming on Netflix.

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:12.234
And his new show, "Poker Face,"
is streaming on Peacock,

00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:14.200
with new episodes
released on Thursdays.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.334
Please welcome to the show
Rian Johnson, everybody.

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:19.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:32.801
How are you?
-I'm doing great, man.

00:00:32.801 --> 00:00:34.033
Thank you so much for having me.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.501
-I'm very excited to be
talking to you about

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:37.000
two different things that
you've recently worked on.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.567
So, obviously,
we have "Glass Onion,"

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:40.467
which is the
"Knives Out" murder mystery,

00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:43.100
and now this wonderful new show
with Natasha Lyonne.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.334
So, one's a murder mystery.

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:47.267
One's a sort of
case-of-the-week-style show.

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.033
But they do seem
so intricly --

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:51.467
intricately, excuse me,
plotted that I'm wondering,

00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:53.834
when you write these,
does that then make you better

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:55.434
at solving a murder mystery

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.267
if you're watching it
in someone else's movie?

00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:59.767
-Oh, my God, no. No.
-Okay.

00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:03.434
If I can pause the movie
and go away for eight months

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:06.367
and write the rest,
then I can figure it out.

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:08.767
But, no, I'm incredibly bad.

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:11.167
But that's actually -- I feel
like that's part of the way

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:15.534
I kind of approach these things
is, to me, always remembering

00:01:15.534 --> 00:01:17.667
that when I do watch
a murder mystery,

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:19.801
I'm trying to figure out
and solve it

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:21.467
maybe for the first like
20 minutes,

00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:24.167
and then I realize,
"I'm never going to solve this."

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:26.200
And I just wait for
the amazing solution

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.434
the detective gives at the end.

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:29.434
So the way I approach
writing these things is

00:01:29.434 --> 00:01:31.934
remembering that and remembering
that the entertaining thing

00:01:31.934 --> 00:01:34.534
isn't the audience
solving a puzzle.

00:01:34.534 --> 00:01:36.367
It's got to be a roller coaster.
It's got to be funny.

00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:37.534
It's got to be thrilling.

00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:40.467
It's got to keep you engaged
moment to moment.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:41.834
And then it's kind of
a bonus at the end

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:43.934
that you get kind of
a puzzle reveal.

00:01:43.934 --> 00:01:45.968
So -- Yeah, so,
being bad at them

00:01:45.968 --> 00:01:48.968
helps me is what I'm
trying to sell here, yeah.

00:01:48.968 --> 00:01:50.300
-One thing they
have in common is,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.467
I do think when you
talk about it being fun

00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:54.901
and it being a roller coaster,
the people solving it

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:56.300
on-screen have to be
fun to watch.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.968
You certainly have that
with Daniel Craig.

00:01:57.968 --> 00:01:59.801
You have that with
Natasha Lyonne.

00:01:59.801 --> 00:02:02.634
I've heard you refer to
the movie as a whodunit

00:02:02.634 --> 00:02:04.767
and the TV show as a howdunit.

00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:07.601
Can you explain the differences
between those two?

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:10.968
-You're asking the right man.
I can do that.

00:02:10.968 --> 00:02:14.667
Yeah, so, whodunit, you've got
whatever, eight, nine suspects.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.033
Any one of them
could have done it.

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:19.334
At the end, kapow,
it was this person, oh, my God.

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:22.434
How-catch-'em, which is how
I kind of have heard it

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:23.801
referred to -- Like, "Columbo"

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:26.167
is the most classic
kind of example of that.

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:27.634
And that's what we're doing
with "Poker Face,"

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.634
where we show you,
in the first act,

00:02:29.634 --> 00:02:32.100
exactly who did it
and how they did it.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.601
And then the whole thing
is kind of this

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:35.767
"Crime and Punishment"
cat-and-mouse game

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:39.067
between them and Natasha Lyonne,
how she's going to catch them.

00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:40.501
And it's fun.

00:02:40.501 --> 00:02:42.133
I mean, especially in
the hour-long format,

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:44.868
what that means is, when we get
great guest stars like --

00:02:44.868 --> 00:02:47.367
We have, like, Nick Nolte
and Cherry Jones in an episode.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:48.767
We've got Lil Rel Howery.

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:50.033
We've got, like,
an incredible --

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:52.267
That means that the entire
episode, they can own it.

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:54.067
We don't have to service
eight different people.

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:55.834
So it's great for the format,
you know.

00:02:55.834 --> 00:02:57.834
-Certainly from
where I'm sitting,

00:02:57.834 --> 00:03:00.100
you seem like
a very prolific writer.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.734
Do you consider yourself someone
who enjoys the craft of writing?

00:03:03.734 --> 00:03:06.400
-Oh, my God, no.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.434
-By the way, thank you so much
for your honesty.

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:12.501
I feel like not enough
successful writers admit this.

00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:15.834
-I feel so grateful.

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:20.067
I love what I do so much,
and that coexists perfectly with

00:03:20.067 --> 00:03:22.000
absolutely being
miserable every moment

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.300
that I'm supposed to be writing.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:24.567
And it's -- I don't know.

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:26.567
Do you -- When you write,
it's just procrastination.

00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:28.033
-Oh, I hate it.

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:30.968
-It's guilt and misery, and
you're alone and eating poorly.

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:34.534
-Yeah. And then I would imagine
you have this

00:03:34.534 --> 00:03:36.634
because you have to sort of
crack things, right?

00:03:36.634 --> 00:03:37.934
Especially with
a murder mystery.

00:03:37.934 --> 00:03:40.133
When you come up with
a solution to a problem

00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:42.200
you've created for yourself,
do you get --

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:43.567
does your heart
start racing a little bit?

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:45.501
Do you get excited knowing,
"Oh, my gosh.

00:03:45.501 --> 00:03:46.767
I think I've done it."

00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:49.801
-You get maybe two of those
per writing process.

00:03:49.801 --> 00:03:51.501
So over the course of a year,
there may be

00:03:51.501 --> 00:03:54.334
two little moments of,
"Ah, eureka,"

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:55.934
and the rest of it, it's just...

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:57.400
-Yeah.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.534
But isn't it -- But now let's
look at the other side of it.

00:03:59.534 --> 00:04:01.000
Right now, here you are.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.367
All you get to do, you know,
for the time you're out here

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:05.434
is talk about two things
you have finished.

00:04:05.434 --> 00:04:06.567
Isn't that nice?

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:07.834
We're talking about
writing that's done.

00:04:07.834 --> 00:04:08.968
Isn't that the best thing
in the world?

00:04:08.968 --> 00:04:11.501
-I hate writing.
I love having written, yes.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.734
When I can put on a suit
and come talk to you,

00:04:13.734 --> 00:04:15.234
this is my happy place.

00:04:15.234 --> 00:04:17.534
-Yeah. I'm assuming this is not
the suit you wear to write in.

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:18.667
-Every single day, man.

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:20.667
I'm like, this is...

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:24.434
-Now, your wife
used to be a film critic.

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:27.067
So, obviously, she has a --
you know, a very sharp eye

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.934
for what is good and bad
on-screen.

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:32.200
Does that mean that she gives
you feedback on your work?

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.434
-Well, yeah, my wife,
Karina Longworth,

00:04:34.434 --> 00:04:36.100
she was a really successful
film critic.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.367
She's now a film historian.

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.601
She has a podcast called
"You Must Remember This"

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:42.267
that's an incredible
Hollywood podcast.

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:45.634
And she also has
an incredible ability --

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.234
I think this is a skill.

00:04:47.234 --> 00:04:50.501
She's absolutely incapable
of feigning interest

00:04:50.501 --> 00:04:52.868
in something
she doesn't find interesting,

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:55.234
which I'm trying to
get better at, actually.

00:04:55.234 --> 00:04:57.400
I've kept myself in
so many awkward situations

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.334
by feigning interest and, like,
because I thought it was polite.

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:03.834
And so -- But what that means
is, I -- And this is good.

00:05:03.834 --> 00:05:05.367
I have to polish something
and make sure

00:05:05.367 --> 00:05:08.434
it's really good before
I pitch it to her,

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:11.167
because I want her
to love it so much, yes.

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:12.434
-That's great.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:12.434 --> 00:05:15.367
-I mean, I do think, ultimately,
if you had to choose

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:17.234
one or the other, you would
rather have someone you trust

00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:18.434
than someone who tells you
everything you're doing

00:05:18.434 --> 00:05:19.567
is great.

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:21.100
-Maybe, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.234
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:23.501
Depending on what phase
in the writing.

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:26.400
-Right. Exactly.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.667
It must be so -- You know,
we're a huge fan,

00:05:29.667 --> 00:05:31.534
good friends with Natasha
here on this show.

00:05:31.534 --> 00:05:33.367
She's coming on tomorrow,
which I'm excited about.

00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:34.634
Have you enjoyed
collaborating with her?

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:36.200
Because she has
a very unique voice.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.467
-Oh, my God.
She's been the best.

00:05:37.467 --> 00:05:38.968
And this is -- I mean,
that's been the fun thing

00:05:38.968 --> 00:05:41.734
about this show is it's not like
I made the show and cast her.

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:44.200
This has been, you know,
a real collaboration.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.234
And anyone who's
seen the show knows the show

00:05:46.234 --> 00:05:48.501
is kind of made for her
like a bespoke suit.

00:05:48.501 --> 00:05:50.934
It's really --
Everything about it is designed

00:05:50.934 --> 00:05:53.434
because I love Natasha,

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:55.667
and it's all designed
to kind of strap a rocket

00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:57.534
to the back of
what I love about Natasha

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:59.300
and get it straight to people,
so, yeah.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.434
-Is there anything
you and Natasha do on set

00:06:01.434 --> 00:06:04.067
that would be in line
with what we might expect from

00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:05.367
someone like Natasha Lyonne?

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:07.868
-[ Laughs ]
Every single moment.

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:10.200
You know, one thing that --
So, one thing --

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.567
And this is fun
because this sounds absurd,

00:06:12.567 --> 00:06:15.400
but there's a method behind the
madness, like much of Natasha.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.334
We would -- In between takes,
we would start

00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:20.934
driving the crew nuts by singing
the Meow Mix song to each other.

00:06:20.934 --> 00:06:22.734
And then we started --
Just, like, any song

00:06:22.734 --> 00:06:24.234
that would come on the radio,
for the next hour,

00:06:24.234 --> 00:06:25.934
it would just be, like,
Led Zeppelin

00:06:25.934 --> 00:06:27.200
done in the Meow Mix --

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:28.734
♪ Meow, meow, meow, meow ♪

00:06:28.734 --> 00:06:30.868
Which -- But the thing is,
what she's --

00:06:30.868 --> 00:06:32.567
I realized
it's actually important,

00:06:32.567 --> 00:06:33.934
what she's doing there.

00:06:33.934 --> 00:06:35.868
When you're making a movie,
there's so much carpentry,

00:06:35.868 --> 00:06:38.834
and it's very easy to
get lost in the,

00:06:38.834 --> 00:06:40.834
"We get this shot,
then we get this shot."

00:06:40.834 --> 00:06:43.634
And her job
is to create something

00:06:43.634 --> 00:06:46.501
that feels alive and spontaneous
in front of the camera.

00:06:46.501 --> 00:06:49.934
And so when she does that or
when she riffs or makes jokes,

00:06:49.934 --> 00:06:51.534
what she's doing,
it actually has a real purpose.

00:06:51.534 --> 00:06:52.734
It's very smart.

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:55.067
It's keeping that
element of chaos alive

00:06:55.067 --> 00:06:57.000
in between the takes
so it will be there

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.167
when we roll the cameras.

00:06:58.167 --> 00:06:59.300
-It's very interesting
you point that out,

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.534
because, of course,
Natasha, on-screen,

00:07:01.534 --> 00:07:05.200
has always made it look like it
just comes so naturally.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.033
But, of course,
it has to be a controlled chaos.

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:09.300
Nobody -- Even that,
once it's on film,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.033
it has to be considered,
it has to be a craft,

00:07:11.033 --> 00:07:12.300
and she's so good at it.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.667
-I mean, Natasha
and also, you know,

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:16.334
Daniel Craig as Benoit Blanc.

00:07:16.334 --> 00:07:18.767
I mean, I've learned over
the past few years,

00:07:18.767 --> 00:07:23.033
by observation,
if a performance feels breezy

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:25.767
and looks effortless, the actor
put a hell of a lot of work

00:07:25.767 --> 00:07:26.968
into making that happen.

00:07:26.968 --> 00:07:29.167
And having those folks,
being able to learn from

00:07:29.167 --> 00:07:30.801
watching that
has been pretty cool.

00:07:30.801 --> 00:07:33.667
-I should stress, like I said,
I also hate writing,

00:07:33.667 --> 00:07:36.167
but the longer I worked with
the cast when I was on "SNL,"

00:07:36.167 --> 00:07:37.434
like, the more you learn
their voices.

00:07:37.434 --> 00:07:39.834
It didn't get easy,
but it got a little easier.

00:07:39.834 --> 00:07:41.434
I got to imagine
the same thing happens

00:07:41.434 --> 00:07:44.067
when you write for Daniel
a second time as Benoit Blanc,

00:07:44.067 --> 00:07:45.601
when you write for Natasha
in current episodes

00:07:45.601 --> 00:07:47.067
after seeing what she can do.

00:07:47.067 --> 00:07:49.801
Does it get a little bit easier
knowing they can pull off stuff?

00:07:49.801 --> 00:07:52.100
-Does it? I'm going to hope.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.100
I'm going to -- I'm going to
hope you're right.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.734
At some point,
this gets a little --

00:07:56.734 --> 00:07:59.467
Yeah, I guess it kind of does,
but it also gets harder,

00:07:59.467 --> 00:08:01.667
because the thing I described
in Natasha with the Meow,

00:08:01.667 --> 00:08:03.934
I'm trying to do my own Meow Mix
when I'm writing,

00:08:03.934 --> 00:08:05.634
because the last thing
I want to do

00:08:05.634 --> 00:08:07.801
writing another Benoit Blanc
movie is to have it

00:08:07.801 --> 00:08:10.968
be a photocopy of
his performance in the last one.

00:08:10.968 --> 00:08:12.868
-Yeah, that's true.
Setting a high bar for yourself.

00:08:12.868 --> 00:08:14.033
You blew it.

00:08:14.033 --> 00:08:15.868
You should have
wrote some stinkers first.

00:08:15.868 --> 00:08:18.834
You should have started with
some real bad duds, and then --

00:08:18.834 --> 00:08:20.801
-Where were you
when I needed you? Yeah.

00:08:20.801 --> 00:08:22.067
-Hey, congratulations on
both of them.

00:08:22.067 --> 00:08:23.100
It's such a pleasure
to have you here.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.667
-Thanks so much, Seth.
-I'm such a fan.

00:08:24.667 --> 00:08:26.200
You guys, that's Rian Johnson.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:27.968
"Glass Onion"
is streaming on Netflix,

00:08:27.968 --> 00:08:30.834
and new episodes of "Poker Face"
stream Thursdays on Peacock.

00:08:30.834 --> 00:08:32.467
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

